    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 072330\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n430 PM PDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through Friday, with \n  dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Chances for gusty winds, cooler temperatures, and potentially\n  widespread precipitation arrive Sunday through Tuesday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through early next week.\n\nHigh pressure persists over the next few days, maintaining hot and \ndry conditions across the forecast area. Afternoon high temperatures \nwill be 10 to 15 degrees above normal for early October, including \nhighs in the upper 90s for Las Vegas. Highs in the upper 100s yield \nMajor Heat Risk in Death Valley and an Extreme Heat Warning remains \nin effect through Friday. Multiple daily high temperature and warm \nminimum temperature records are in jeopardy - see the CLIMATE \nsection below for details. \n\nAttention turns to a major pattern shift this weekend involving an \nupper level trough, a weak low, and Hurricane Rachel. First, \nHurricane Rachel, currently located 120W 20N, has been moving west \nnorthwest under the influence of high pressure over the western \nUnited States and Mexico. However, the system will take a sharp turn \nto the northeast with the approach of a cutoff low near California. \nInteraction between these two features including how far west the \nthe two track and whether the combined systems remain a closed low \nat all can result in many different outcomes. Confidence decreases \nfurther with the approach of the main trough as it moves down the \nWest Coast. Remnant moisture from Rachel will be pulled into the \ntroughing pattern and fuel precipitation across the southwestern \nUnited States. With this in mind, speed and placement of the \nmoisture infused trough will have a great influence on rainfall \namounts. Should the trough move quickly, moisture will be pushed \neast and precipitation amounts will be light. A slower moving system \nwill allow higher precipitation amounts, and trajectory of the \nmoisture may boost totals over certain mountain ranges. Cold \ntemperatures aloft under the low may support particularly heavy \nrainfall amounts at times. \n\nAs it stands now, the most likely scenario places the highest \nprecipitation amounts on Sunday afternoon through Sunday night over \nMohave, Lincoln, Clark, and eastern San Bernardino counties. Upslope \nflow particularly favors the Spring Mountains, Sheep Range, and \nvarious mountain ranges in Lincoln County. Lighter precipitation \nwill linger Monday onward, depending on how fast the trough tracks \neast. Besides precipitation, southerly to southwesterly winds with \npeak gusts between 35 and 45 mph pick up on Saturday and Sunday with \nthe approach of the trough. Colder temperatures are also on track, \nwith highs decreasing 25 to 30 degrees between Friday and Tuesday. \nTemperatures may become cold enough to put a few inches of snow over \nthe Sierra Crest. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds under 8KT following typical daytime and nighttime directions \nare expected through the period. VFR skies with afternoon FEW-SCT \nclouds around 12kft.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds follow typical \ndaily directional patterns with speeds of 10KT or less for most \nlocations. The exception will be KIFP, where breezy northerly winds \nincluding gusts to around 20KT will persist this afternoon then pick \nup again Thursday afternoon. VFR conditions are expected areawide \nwith cloud bases at or above 10kft. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE....Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     99(1978)*   98(2024)*   97(2024)*\nBishop        94(1965)    95(1996)    93(2015)\nNeedles       107(2024)   107(2024)   104(1996)*\nDaggett       102(2024)   101(1996)*  103(1980)\nKingman       99(2024)    98(1996)    98(1910)\nDesert Rock   99(2024)    98(1996)*   97(1996)\nDeath Valley  109(2024)*  112(1996)*  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     76(2024)*   72(2024)*\nBishop        51(1960)*   61(2025)\nNeedles       76(2024)*   76(1988)*\nDaggett       69(1980)*   69(1996)*\nKingman       67(1960)*   68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   70(2024)    65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  85(1964)*   78(2024)*\n\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/AVIATION...Meltzer\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
